---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Back-of-the-envelope numbers" hint="How long a commit waits for clock uncertainty compared with the network, how many commits per second one hot row allows, and how many replica failures a group survives. A teaching model with round numbers, not Google's data.">
  <div class="pg est" data-est>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <RangeField label="Clock uncertainty, epsilon (ms)" name="e" min="1" max="20" value="4" />
      <RangeField label="Round trip to the nearest majority of replicas (ms)" name="t" min="1" max="100" value="10" />
      <RangeField label="Paxos groups in the transaction" name="p" min="1" max="100" value="1" />
      <RangeField label="Replicas per Paxos group" name="s" min="3" max="7" step="2" value="5" />
    </div>
    <dl class="est-out" data-out aria-live="polite"></dl>
    <p class="pg-note">Teaching model, not Google's system or data. From the Spanner paper: uncertainty is about 4 ms most of the time and generally under 10 ms, the expected commit wait is at least twice the uncertainty and is typically overlapped with Paxos communication, leaders hold leases that default to 10 seconds, and F1 used five replicas. The round trip is a guess you can change. Assumptions: one Paxos round on the commit path for a single group and two when the transaction spans groups (prepare, then commit), the wait runs alongside those rounds, and locks are held until the commit finishes so transactions on one row run one after another. Real commits also include lock acquisition, logging and the time to reach the leader.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue, formatOneDecimalBelow100 } from '../../playground';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-est]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = numberValue(q);
    const OUT_ROWS = 10; // lines in the result list
    const rows = Array.from({ length: OUT_ROWS }, () => { const dt = document.createElement('dt'); const dd = document.createElement('dd'); q('[data-out]').append(dt, dd); return { dt, dd }; });
    const f1 = formatOneDecimalBelow100;
    const pct = (n: number) => `${(Math.round(n * 1000) / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')}%`;
    function draw() {
      ['e', 't', 'p', 's'].forEach((key) => (q(`[data-${key}val]`).textContent = String(val(`[data-${key}]`))));
      const eps = val('[data-e]'); const rtt = val('[data-t]'); const groups = val('[data-p]'); const replicas = val('[data-s]');
      const wait = 2 * eps; const rounds = groups > 1 ? 2 : 1; const paxos = rounds * rtt;
      const commit = Math.max(paxos, wait); const extra = Math.max(0, wait - paxos);
      const majority = Math.floor(replicas / 2) + 1;
      const data: [string, string][] = [
        ['Commit wait, at least (ms)', f1(wait)],
        ['Paxos rounds on the commit path', String(rounds)],
        ['Time for those rounds (ms)', f1(paxos)],
        ['Commit latency, with the wait overlapping the rounds (ms)', f1(commit)],
        ['Extra delay caused only by clock uncertainty (ms)', f1(extra)],
        ['Share of commit latency spent only waiting for clocks', `${f1((100 * extra) / commit)}%`],
        ['Most commits per second on one hot row', f1(1000 / commit)],
        ['Replicas needed for a majority', String(majority)],
        ['Replica failures one group survives', String(replicas - majority)],
        ['Longest wait for a new leader after a leader dies (seconds, default lease)', '10'],
      ];
      rows.forEach((r, i) => { r.dt.textContent = data[i][0]; r.dd.textContent = data[i][1]; });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
